WEBVTT

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:03.940
This needle has pointed
north your whole life.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.900
But the seafloor remembers further back.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.450
North and south have swapped.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:12.800
Again and again, with no schedule.

00:00:13.537 --> 00:00:16.997
That needle follows a field
that wraps the whole planet.

00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:18.797
It feels permanent.

00:00:19.217 --> 00:00:20.417
It is not.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:24.500
At ocean ridges, fresh
lava cools into rock.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:28.360
Tiny magnetic grains lock
in the field's direction.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:32.990
The seafloor spreads,
and each flip starts a new stripe.

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:38.940
Five million years, about twenty
flips, and wildly uneven gaps.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.530
So, two questions.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.580
When will it flip next?

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:45.360
And how often does it flip?

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:47.590
Which one can we answer?

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:54.400
In a simple computer model of a
flipping field, the first is hopeless.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.180
The second is not.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.520
Finding out why takes us
through liquid metal and chaos.

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:04.710
Then to machines that dream up
snapshots of magnetic fields.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.920
They must obey some laws exactly,
and others only on average.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.180
How often does the field flip?

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:18.690
A learner's map of the MilaDy project,
from a compass to today's research.

00:01:21.237 --> 00:01:23.782
Should the first
simulations target how often

00:01:23.862 --> 00:01:27.517
the field flips, or the warning
signs just before a flip?

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:35.520
Is there a giant magnet
hidden inside the Earth?

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.080
Deep down, the outer core
is an ocean of liquid iron.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:45.470
Left alone, a field there would fade
away within tens of thousands of years.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.920
Yet Earth's field is
several billion years old.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.960
So the moving iron must keep
making it, all the time.

00:01:54.003 --> 00:01:58.113
Take a bar magnet: north at
one end, south at the other.

00:01:58.393 --> 00:01:59.593
Cut it in half.

00:01:59.673 --> 00:02:03.353
You get two magnets, each
with a north and a south.

00:02:03.833 --> 00:02:07.043
No one has ever found
a lone magnetic pole.

00:02:07.783 --> 00:02:11.573
Field lines say the same:
they never start or stop.

00:02:12.073 --> 00:02:14.203
Draw any box, and count.

00:02:14.393 --> 00:02:16.803
As many lines go in as come out.

00:02:17.183 --> 00:02:18.923
Keep this box in mind.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:25.010
A bicycle dynamo turns motion into
electricity, using a built-in magnet.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.470
The Earth has to make its
own. The trick: a loop.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:33.880
Hold a microphone near its own
speaker, and turn up the gain.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.330
Below a certain point, a whisper fades.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.410
Past it, the loop feeds itself.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:46.760
Now spin a copper disc wired
to a coil around its axle.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.920
Current makes field,
and field makes current.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:54.286
Spin it faster and faster.
Does the field grow bit by bit,

00:02:54.366 --> 00:02:56.890
or not at all until some speed?

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:03.030
Not at all, until a threshold.

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.228
Past it, each turn multiplies a tiny

00:03:06.308 --> 00:03:10.580
seed field by the same factor:
exponential growth.

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.180
Just past the threshold, growth is slow.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:18.270
Then the field brakes the
disc, and growth stops.

00:03:19.503 --> 00:03:21.063
Who pays for all this?

00:03:21.463 --> 00:03:25.873
A motor drives the flow;
in the Earth, convection does.

00:03:26.313 --> 00:03:30.353
The flow stretches the field;
the field brakes the flow.

00:03:30.813 --> 00:03:36.323
Once growth stops, every watt that
goes in leaves as heat, on average.

00:03:36.633 --> 00:03:41.333
A dynamo turns motion into
field; it creates no energy.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:46.840
Earth's field points one way today.
Which way should it prefer?

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.480
Reverse every field line:
the dynamo's equations read the same.

00:03:54.790 --> 00:04:00.190
So, neither. The two directions
are mirror twins, equally lawful.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:06.460
Which one you get is history, not law:
in the disc, the seed's sign decides.

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:09.330
And nothing in the laws forbids a flip.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:15.420
Moving iron, a loop with a threshold,
no favourite direction.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:19.460
But the disc had wires;
liquid iron has none.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:23.040
Then what motion can do it, and how fast?

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:27.321
Near the threshold,
should dynamo simulations

00:04:27.401 --> 00:04:30.830
start from a tiny seed,
a strong one, or both?

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:39.040
What kind of motion can
grow a magnetic field?

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:45.030
In a perfect conductor, field lines
would be threads frozen into the fluid.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:49.840
Wherever the flow stretches a thread,
the field along it grows stronger.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:54.140
Real metals let the threads
smudge, like spreading ink.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:58.130
The smudging speed is set by the
metal's magnetic diffusivity.

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.560
Same flow, two metals.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:04.700
A good conductor smudges slowly:
the threads stay sharp.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.040
A poor one smudges fast:
the threads blur away.

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:15.060
One number keeps the score:
the magnetic Reynolds number, Rm.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:21.030
It is the flow's speed, times its size,
divided by the magnetic diffusivity.

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:25.300
Large Rm: stretching outpaces smudging.

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:28.640
Small Rm: smudging wins.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:33.370
Turn the Rm knob up,
and watch a weak seed field.

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:36.840
At low Rm, the seed dies away.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:43.190
Past a threshold, the growth rate crosses
zero and the seed grows, like the squeal.

00:05:44.503 --> 00:05:47.163
Laboratory dynamos use liquid sodium.

00:05:47.443 --> 00:05:49.653
It flows as easily as water.

00:05:49.963 --> 00:05:54.873
But a field smudges in it about a hundred
thousand times faster than its swirls do.

00:05:55.203 --> 00:06:01.203
Their ratio, one in a hundred thousand,
is the magnetic Prandtl number, Pm.

00:06:02.063 --> 00:06:05.933
Reaching an Rm of a few
tens takes violent stirring.

00:06:06.333 --> 00:06:11.023
The flow's Reynolds number, the gauge
of turbulence, reaches millions.

00:06:11.213 --> 00:06:14.303
So every laboratory dynamo is turbulent.

00:06:15.337 --> 00:06:17.616
Only three liquid-sodium experiments have

00:06:17.696 --> 00:06:20.557
made their own field,
each a different kind.

00:06:21.037 --> 00:06:26.297
In Riga, a corkscrew flow made a field
that oscillates and slowly turns.

00:06:26.537 --> 00:06:32.537
In Karlsruhe, fifty-two spiralling cells
made a steady field, close to a dipole.

00:06:32.947 --> 00:06:37.727
And only VKS, at Cadarache,
made a dipole that can flip.

00:06:38.467 --> 00:06:42.070
A fourth, DRESDYN in Dresden,
aims to join them:

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:45.857
a spinning cylinder
whose axis slowly turns.

00:06:46.903 --> 00:06:52.413
Inside VKS: a cylinder holding a hundred
and fifty litres of liquid sodium.

00:06:52.833 --> 00:06:58.693
At its ends, two bladed discs, the
impellers, spin in opposite directions.

00:06:59.433 --> 00:07:02.113
Should it matter what metal
the discs are made of?

00:07:05.353 --> 00:07:10.143
With stainless steel discs, there
was no dynamo, even at full speed.

00:07:10.443 --> 00:07:15.473
Swap in discs of soft iron,
and a dipole grows along the axis.

00:07:17.213 --> 00:07:21.803
So the threshold depends on more
than Rm: materials count too.

00:07:22.837 --> 00:07:25.677
Now spin the two discs
at different speeds.

00:07:26.217 --> 00:07:30.947
At some settings, the dipole flips
again and again, with no schedule.

00:07:31.277 --> 00:07:34.947
Like the compass needle at the
start, but every minute or two.

00:07:35.687 --> 00:07:38.877
Each flip itself takes
only about five seconds.

00:07:39.257 --> 00:07:45.257
Uneven stripes, like the seafloor's, but
written in minutes, not millions of years.

00:07:46.403 --> 00:07:49.215
Simulations cannot reach sodium's tiny Pm:

00:07:49.295 --> 00:07:53.893
which Pm values should the first
three-dimensional runs cover?

00:07:59.337 --> 00:08:04.357
Riga, Karlsruhe and VKS crowned
a hunt of nearly a century.

00:08:04.707 --> 00:08:05.917
Here is its map.

00:08:06.657 --> 00:08:12.657
Lane one, the Earth's record: lavas
magnetized backwards, nineteen oh six.

00:08:13.257 --> 00:08:16.477
Seafloor stripes, nineteen sixty-three.

00:08:16.777 --> 00:08:21.467
Then flips as chance events,
whose odds change over the ages.

00:08:22.503 --> 00:08:24.443
Lane two, theory.

00:08:24.833 --> 00:08:30.493
Nineteen nineteen: a moving, conducting
fluid might keep the Sun's field alive.

00:08:30.933 --> 00:08:36.913
Nineteen thirty-three: no flow can sustain
a field that is symmetric around an axis.

00:08:37.653 --> 00:08:42.293
Nineteen fifty-eight: the first
proven examples of flows that can.

00:08:42.583 --> 00:08:45.319
In the seventies came simple dynamo flows,

00:08:45.399 --> 00:08:48.613
later the blueprints
of Riga and Karlsruhe.

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:52.530
Lane three: building a dynamo.

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:57.740
Nineteen sixty-three: a model
dynamo of spinning iron cylinders.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:03.960
Nineteen ninety-nine: sodium makes its
own field, in Riga, then Karlsruhe.

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:08.603
Two thousand six: VKS,
only with iron discs,

00:09:08.683 --> 00:09:13.300
then the first flips of a
turbulent sodium dynamo.

00:09:14.737 --> 00:09:16.877
Lane four, computers.

00:09:17.337 --> 00:09:21.627
Nineteen eighty-one: simulated
turbulence grows dynamos.

00:09:22.127 --> 00:09:27.067
Nineteen ninety-five: a simulated
Earth's core reverses its field.

00:09:27.477 --> 00:09:31.917
Ringed pins mark papers co-signed
by this project's supervisors.

00:09:32.567 --> 00:09:38.017
Nineteen ninety-seven: two simulated
counter-rotating eddies make a dynamo.

00:09:38.377 --> 00:09:43.627
Two thousand five: such dynamos
still work at a Pm of one hundredth.

00:09:44.367 --> 00:09:50.367
From twenty sixteen, two codes simulate
the VKS tank, iron impellers included.

00:09:51.167 --> 00:09:53.613
Twenty twenty-six: both make it flip,

00:09:53.693 --> 00:09:57.777
in the runs explored only
with conducting surroundings.

00:09:58.027 --> 00:10:03.467
Their reference run: a Reynolds number
near sixteen hundred, not millions.

00:10:04.503 --> 00:10:07.833
Lane five: a few dials instead of a tank.

00:10:08.393 --> 00:10:12.893
Nineteen fifty-eight: two coupled
disc dynamos already flip.

00:10:13.383 --> 00:10:19.383
Twenty twelve: three dials that flip by
chaos alone; we will play with them soon.

00:10:20.413 --> 00:10:25.063
Twenty twenty-six: four dials
fitted to the simulated VKS flips,

00:10:25.143 --> 00:10:28.503
by a method that finds equations in data.

00:10:29.537 --> 00:10:31.566
MilaDy began in twenty twenty-six,

00:10:31.646 --> 00:10:35.947
bringing machine learning where
tanks, computers and dials meet.

00:10:36.537 --> 00:10:40.331
Yet the data-driven lane is
thin: in twenty twenty-two,

00:10:40.411 --> 00:10:46.411
the learning methods one study tried could
not reliably foresee a geomagnetic flip.

00:10:47.327 --> 00:10:50.445
The internship and a
proposed thesis add a bet:

00:10:50.525 --> 00:10:54.937
machines that dream up dynamo
fields and obey their physics.

00:10:55.677 --> 00:11:00.487
Behind every lane, the same race:
stretching against smudging.

00:11:04.737 --> 00:11:09.787
Back to the race: the flow stretches
the field, and diffusion smudges it.

00:11:10.147 --> 00:11:13.987
Physicists write the very same
race as a single equation.

00:11:14.727 --> 00:11:19.397
On the left: how fast the magnetic
field changes, at each point.

00:11:20.097 --> 00:11:22.746
First term on the right:
the flow carries the

00:11:22.826 --> 00:11:26.007
field along and stretches
it, like the threads.

00:11:26.537 --> 00:11:32.327
Second term: diffusion smudges it,
at a pace set by the magnetic diffusivity.

00:11:32.807 --> 00:11:35.497
A poor conductor smudges fast.

00:11:35.837 --> 00:11:40.207
Stretching against smudging:
the whole race, in one line.

00:11:41.237 --> 00:11:45.197
Picture the flow as a conveyor
belt that does not feel its cargo.

00:11:45.647 --> 00:11:51.277
A weak field rides on the imposed flow,
and fades or grows exponentially.

00:11:51.707 --> 00:11:56.287
On a log scale: a straight line,
whose slope is the growth rate.

00:11:57.027 --> 00:11:59.777
But a strong field
pushes back on the flow,

00:11:59.857 --> 00:12:04.137
as it braked the copper disc:
the line bends into a plateau.

00:12:04.477 --> 00:12:07.477
These are the kinematic
and dynamic regimes.

00:12:07.697 --> 00:12:09.887
Flips live on the plateau.

00:12:10.937 --> 00:12:14.324
To test ideas, physicists
need a flow simple enough

00:12:14.404 --> 00:12:17.997
to write on one line,
yet able to make a dynamo.

00:12:18.397 --> 00:12:21.787
One classic choice: the ABC flow.

00:12:22.527 --> 00:12:25.567
It fills space with
identical twisting rooms.

00:12:25.897 --> 00:12:28.758
Three crossed winds make
the fluid corkscrew:

00:12:28.838 --> 00:12:31.717
it spins around the direction it moves.

00:12:32.077 --> 00:12:34.787
This box returns higher on our climb.

00:12:35.837 --> 00:12:41.837
Now turn one knob on this flow: Rm.
Turning it up means less diffusion.

00:12:42.457 --> 00:12:45.107
Less smudging should
only help the stretching.

00:12:45.847 --> 00:12:49.887
So once the dynamo switches
on, it stays on. Right?

00:12:53.627 --> 00:12:56.827
Here is the growth rate,
computed at twenty-four settings.

00:12:57.237 --> 00:13:03.237
Below about nine, every field fades. Then
growth turns positive: the dynamo is on.

00:13:04.467 --> 00:13:07.457
Near seventeen and a half,
it switches off again.

00:13:07.787 --> 00:13:10.447
Above about twenty-seven, it comes back.

00:13:10.527 --> 00:13:15.947
Not one threshold, but windows: turning
a knob further does not always help.

00:13:16.197 --> 00:13:19.202
It agrees with published
results. No machine

00:13:19.282 --> 00:13:22.487
learning yet: this only tests the solver.

00:13:23.737 --> 00:13:27.167
Real dynamos have not
one knob, but a panel.

00:13:27.477 --> 00:13:30.927
How hard the metal is stirred;
how well it conducts.

00:13:31.107 --> 00:13:34.127
What the discs are made
of: remember the iron.

00:13:34.367 --> 00:13:39.087
How unequal their speeds are:
that is when VKS flipped.

00:13:39.387 --> 00:13:44.497
For DRESDYN, a precessing tank:
how fast its axis turns.

00:13:45.237 --> 00:13:49.214
Even the wall counts: in VKS
simulations, changing only its

00:13:49.294 --> 00:13:53.957
magnetic condition raised the
threshold by about forty percent.

00:13:55.003 --> 00:13:59.683
Every turbulent dynamo simulation
in three dimensions is very costly.

00:13:59.853 --> 00:14:02.933
So only a handful of knob
settings get simulated.

00:14:03.403 --> 00:14:09.323
Each one is a mountain of data: a single
snapshot can weigh more than a gigabyte.

00:14:10.063 --> 00:14:12.563
Each run holds only a few flips.

00:14:12.703 --> 00:14:15.613
And between the dots,
there is nothing at all.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:19.140
Those gaps are where this film lives.

00:14:19.750 --> 00:14:22.710
The study behind this
film asks three things.

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:26.924
One: can a machine learn
the whole family of fields,

00:14:27.004 --> 00:14:30.000
even at knob settings between the dots?

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:34.040
Two: can it respect
each law where it holds,

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:38.700
exactly in every field,
or on average over many fields?

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:43.130
Three: can it tell us how
often the field flips?

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:47.013
To find out, the study's
plan climbs a staircase,

00:14:47.093 --> 00:14:50.910
from three numbers to the
shapes of real experiments.

00:14:51.650 --> 00:14:55.364
One honest note: this
independent study is prospective,

00:14:55.444 --> 00:14:58.881
built on small experiments
on an ordinary computer,

00:14:58.961 --> 00:15:03.430
and not endorsed by MilaDy,
its partners or the supervisors.

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:10.080
An equation, windows, a panel
of knobs, a handful of dots.

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:12.512
Full simulations cost too much,

00:15:12.592 --> 00:15:16.720
so we start with a three-dial
caricature that already flips.

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:19.270
Can anyone say when it flips next?

00:15:20.603 --> 00:15:23.393
With only a handful of runs,
should the settings

00:15:23.473 --> 00:15:26.673
be spread evenly,
or packed near each threshold?

00:15:31.903 --> 00:15:36.183
Here is the caricature:
three dials, linked by springs.

00:15:36.503 --> 00:15:39.803
One is the dipole,
the north-south part of the field.

00:15:40.273 --> 00:15:43.463
One is the quadrupole, a four-pole part.

00:15:43.873 --> 00:15:47.933
The third is a flow pattern,
driven by a knob, Gamma.

00:15:48.673 --> 00:15:52.693
Three short equations, from
Gissinger, tie them together.

00:15:53.143 --> 00:15:56.813
Flip both field dials,
and the equations read the same.

00:15:57.233 --> 00:16:01.833
Three dials, three numbers:
the bottom step of the staircase.

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:07.950
Use two dials as a map:
quadrupole across, dipole up.

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:13.270
With the knob low, the state
circles in one whirlpool, and stays.

00:16:13.690 --> 00:16:17.920
A mirror twin circles the
mirror whirlpool: dipole down.

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:22.700
Each start is trapped.
The dipole never changes sign.

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:24.860
Now turn Gamma up.

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:31.220
Just above zero point eight six, the
whirlpools touch: a narrow corridor opens.

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:37.570
The state lingers on one side,
then slips through. The dipole flips.

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:42.600
Nothing here is random. So,
will the flips come like clockwork?

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:49.460
No. Long waits and
short ones, irregularly.

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:53.680
The dipole's sign prints uneven
stripes, like the seafloor.

00:16:54.703 --> 00:16:59.303
Now count: one tick each
time the dipole crosses zero.

00:16:59.753 --> 00:17:04.593
Not every tick is a flip: an excursion
can cross and come straight back.

00:17:05.013 --> 00:17:08.173
Ticks per unit of time: the crossing rate.

00:17:08.913 --> 00:17:12.123
Up to zero point eight six: silence.

00:17:12.523 --> 00:17:16.223
Just above, about two ticks
per thousand time units.

00:17:16.653 --> 00:17:19.633
At Gamma equal to one, about eleven.

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:26.170
Set Gamma at zero point nine, and
launch two copies, one millionth apart.

00:17:26.530 --> 00:17:31.600
We know the state to six decimals.
Can we predict the date of the next flip?

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:38.010
At first, the copies move as one.

00:17:38.220 --> 00:17:41.720
But their gap nearly triples
every eleven time units.

00:17:42.170 --> 00:17:45.870
After about a hundred and fifty,
they disagree completely.

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:50.840
The average wait between crossings
is longer: over two hundred.

00:17:51.310 --> 00:17:55.410
Each extra decimal buys only
about twenty-five more time units.

00:17:55.550 --> 00:18:01.340
So the forecast fades before the average
wait ends. Only the statistics survive.

00:18:02.370 --> 00:18:05.520
The path is lost,
so leave the shutter open.

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:09.820
Each pass leaves a little
light: a glow builds.

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:14.070
A copy started on the other side
first paints the mirror image.

00:18:14.510 --> 00:18:17.840
Given time, both paint the same glow.

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:23.450
Where does the dipole spend its
time? Two humps, the same for both.

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:27.490
This reproducible glow is
the stationary distribution.

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:32.020
Below the threshold, each
start paints its own whirlpool.

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:37.390
Now film the trajectory,
one frame every two time units.

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:39.770
Neighbouring frames look alike.

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:42.180
Cut the film into frames.

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:46.970
Drop them in a shoebox, shake,
and throw away the timestamps.

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:51.750
Pour the box out:
the frames redraw the same glow.

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:56.750
Each frame is a snapshot:
three dial readings, nothing more.

00:18:57.050 --> 00:19:00.070
It carries no timestamp and no time order.

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:04.430
Yet the shoebox, plus the equations,
answers many questions.

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:09.750
Averages, energy budgets, even
how often the dipole crosses zero.

00:19:10.803 --> 00:19:14.743
Pick one photo. Its three
dial readings give its energy.

00:19:15.033 --> 00:19:19.783
From that photo alone, the equations
tell how fast its energy changes.

00:19:20.253 --> 00:19:23.263
Drop one marble per photo at that value.

00:19:24.003 --> 00:19:29.073
Some photos are gaining energy,
others losing it. The pile spreads wide.

00:19:29.293 --> 00:19:31.483
Yet its average sits at zero.

00:19:31.823 --> 00:19:35.113
Energy in equals energy out, on average.

00:19:35.853 --> 00:19:38.333
Like a bank account: over the years,

00:19:38.413 --> 00:19:42.513
income matches spending,
yet each day gains or loses.

00:19:42.863 --> 00:19:48.273
This is a law of the crowd. It holds
for the shoebox, not for each photo.

00:19:49.403 --> 00:19:52.557
How many independent snapshots
does each setting need,

00:19:52.637 --> 00:19:55.183
and how far apart must they be saved?

00:20:00.437 --> 00:20:04.577
A puzzle. How many cars
cross this line each hour?

00:20:04.817 --> 00:20:10.207
No clock. Just one photo from above,
where every speedometer can be read.

00:20:10.947 --> 00:20:14.657
Can one photo, with no time
in it, give you a rate?

00:20:17.897 --> 00:20:21.947
It can. Draw a thin strip around the line.

00:20:22.447 --> 00:20:27.667
A fast car spends little time inside,
so catching one there counts for more.

00:20:28.407 --> 00:20:32.857
Add up the speeds of the cars inside,
and divide by the strip's width.

00:20:33.267 --> 00:20:37.027
On average, that is how
many cars cross per hour.

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:42.430
Back to the shoebox. Spread its
photos out by their dipole reading.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:46.850
The line to cross is zero:
draw a thin strip around it.

00:20:47.590 --> 00:20:50.090
Each photo also needs a speedometer.

00:20:50.490 --> 00:20:53.312
No clock required:
the dipole's equation turns

00:20:53.392 --> 00:20:56.560
the photo's three dial
readings into a speed.

00:20:56.970 --> 00:21:01.340
Up or down, both count,
so keep only its size.

00:21:02.080 --> 00:21:03.871
Add the speeds inside the strip,

00:21:03.951 --> 00:21:07.730
then divide by its width and
by the total number of photos.

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:09.850
Out comes the crossing rate.

00:21:10.330 --> 00:21:13.585
Shrink the strip to nothing,
and for well-behaved

00:21:13.665 --> 00:21:16.990
systems the recipe is
exact: Rice's formula.

00:21:17.440 --> 00:21:21.218
The catch: few photos land
in a thin strip. Here,

00:21:21.298 --> 00:21:24.620
about two dozen out of eight thousand.

00:21:25.670 --> 00:21:27.540
Now test it against the truth.

00:21:27.940 --> 00:21:29.750
At forty-one settings of Gamma,

00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:33.330
long simulations count the
crossings as they happen.

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:38.800
Rice gets only the true snapshots,
shuffled, plus the equations.

00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:41.910
The two curves almost coincide.

00:21:42.370 --> 00:21:45.000
Shade the gap,
and divide by the area under

00:21:45.080 --> 00:21:47.930
the counted curve: the cumulative error.

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:51.850
Four percent near the threshold,
six percent above it.

00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:54.725
A good sign, on three chaotic dials that

00:21:54.805 --> 00:21:57.860
are not even guaranteed
to be well-behaved.

00:21:59.303 --> 00:22:03.633
One caution: Rice counts
crossings, not reversals.

00:22:03.983 --> 00:22:09.043
An excursion across zero and back
adds two crossings, but no reversal.

00:22:09.493 --> 00:22:12.201
To isolate reversals, the PhD plan counts

00:22:12.281 --> 00:22:15.603
crossings of a far level,
on the other side.

00:22:16.837 --> 00:22:19.982
So Rice needs only
snapshots and the equations,

00:22:20.062 --> 00:22:23.137
but plenty of snapshots, and cheap ones.

00:22:23.577 --> 00:22:28.047
Yet real dynamo simulations exist
at only a few knob settings.

00:22:28.487 --> 00:22:31.867
Between the dots,
there is no shoebox at all.

00:22:32.327 --> 00:22:35.827
How do you get snapshots at
a setting nobody simulated?

00:22:37.370 --> 00:22:40.029
For VKS, which dipole should Rice count,

00:22:40.109 --> 00:22:44.000
and what threshold turns a
crossing into a reversal?

00:22:49.237 --> 00:22:54.557
New snapshots start as pure noise:
a round cloud of random points.

00:22:54.997 --> 00:23:00.076
Over it, the crowd we want:
one point per snapshot. Here,

00:23:00.156 --> 00:23:04.287
two mirror blobs, like the two whirlpools.

00:23:05.027 --> 00:23:07.507
Now let a wind blow through the cloud.

00:23:07.827 --> 00:23:12.847
Each point drifts, bends,
and lands inside the shape of the data.

00:23:13.147 --> 00:23:17.917
Fresh noise in, fresh snapshots
out, as many as you like.

00:23:18.657 --> 00:23:24.097
That wind is a generative model:
new, plausible examples, on demand.

00:23:24.527 --> 00:23:26.967
The whole game is learning the wind.

00:23:28.003 --> 00:23:29.963
One warning before the formulas.

00:23:30.323 --> 00:23:34.693
The wind runs on its own
transport clock, from zero to one.

00:23:34.983 --> 00:23:40.643
Not physical time: at the end,
you hold one snapshot, not a movie.

00:23:41.383 --> 00:23:47.163
And the wind's arrows move snapshots, not
fluid: they are not the fluid's velocity.

00:23:48.203 --> 00:23:51.433
How is the wind learned?
With a simple trick.

00:23:51.903 --> 00:23:56.833
Keep one coordinate of each point, and
lay the transport clock out to the right.

00:23:57.303 --> 00:24:00.183
Pair each noise point
with a random snapshot;

00:24:00.263 --> 00:24:02.763
join them with a straight string.

00:24:03.013 --> 00:24:06.053
Thousands of strings, crossing everywhere.

00:24:06.793 --> 00:24:09.283
Pick one place, at one moment.

00:24:10.053 --> 00:24:13.563
Several strings pass through,
pointing different ways.

00:24:13.963 --> 00:24:18.213
The network learns one arrow
there: their average direction.

00:24:18.653 --> 00:24:21.523
That is the whole training:
plain regression,

00:24:21.603 --> 00:24:24.293
like fitting a curve through points.

00:24:25.033 --> 00:24:28.173
Follow the averaged wind, not the strings.

00:24:28.423 --> 00:24:32.763
The paths bend, and drawn against
the clock, they never cross.

00:24:33.153 --> 00:24:38.253
Learn the average exactly, and the
noise lands exactly on the data's crowd.

00:24:39.303 --> 00:24:41.103
Now give the wind a knob.

00:24:41.293 --> 00:24:45.393
Turn it: the target changes
shape, and the cloud follows.

00:24:45.583 --> 00:24:47.943
One network, every setting.

00:24:48.683 --> 00:24:52.943
On the three dials, a real network
trained at the filled dots.

00:24:53.203 --> 00:24:56.953
It was tested at the open dots,
never seen in training.

00:24:57.253 --> 00:25:02.593
In between, no theorem promises
success: only tests can tell.

00:25:03.003 --> 00:25:05.793
Beyond the range, nothing was tested.

00:25:06.533 --> 00:25:12.203
At an open dot, the network's snapshots
join the shoebox, beside simulated ones.

00:25:13.237 --> 00:25:18.127
A generated snapshot has no
true twin, so we compare crowds.

00:25:18.547 --> 00:25:21.157
Picture each crowd as a pile of sand.

00:25:21.467 --> 00:25:25.307
A distribution distance scores
how different the two piles are.

00:25:25.677 --> 00:25:30.377
The earth-mover kind counts how
much sand must move, and how far.

00:25:31.117 --> 00:25:35.477
A perfect model, scored against
the data: will it get zero?

00:25:38.717 --> 00:25:43.827
No. Roll a fair die a thousand
times, then a thousand more.

00:25:44.067 --> 00:25:48.517
Same die, same truth,
yet the two histograms differ.

00:25:49.007 --> 00:25:53.697
That gap is the noise floor,
about what a perfect model scores.

00:25:54.097 --> 00:25:59.737
Judge every model against it,
at equal sample size, never against zero.

00:26:00.477 --> 00:26:03.937
One catch: the samples
must be independent.

00:26:04.297 --> 00:26:08.787
Overlap them, and the floor drops
too low, as the report warns.

00:26:09.937 --> 00:26:12.777
Which statistics must the
generated fields match,

00:26:12.857 --> 00:26:16.227
and how close to the noise
floor counts as success?

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:24.220
Physics hands the machine
two kinds of laws.

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:26.980
Some hold for every single snapshot.

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:29.420
Others hold only for the crowd.

00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:32.100
Two shelves, still empty.

00:26:32.840 --> 00:26:37.000
Back to the three dials, at a
Gamma never seen in training.

00:26:37.420 --> 00:26:39.700
Grey: true snapshots.

00:26:40.030 --> 00:26:42.570
Violet: a plain generator.

00:26:42.840 --> 00:26:44.230
The shape is right.

00:26:44.970 --> 00:26:46.540
Now weigh the sides.

00:26:47.060 --> 00:26:50.090
The truth is balanced; the machine is not.

00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:53.620
Only about four in ten of
its snapshots point up.

00:26:54.140 --> 00:26:56.979
It copied its data: below the threshold,

00:26:57.059 --> 00:27:01.840
each run stays in one whirlpool,
and the runs split unevenly.

00:27:02.870 --> 00:27:04.860
Two ways to teach the mirror.

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:07.517
The first student, data augmentation,

00:27:07.597 --> 00:27:11.180
sees each training snapshot
flipped half the time.

00:27:11.540 --> 00:27:15.445
The second, equivariance,
has the mirror built into its wind:

00:27:15.525 --> 00:27:19.840
a mirrored noise point always
lands on the mirrored snapshot.

00:27:20.580 --> 00:27:24.150
At a Gamma neither has seen,
who stays balanced?

00:27:27.890 --> 00:27:29.300
The built-in mirror.

00:27:29.710 --> 00:27:31.640
The first student tilts a little.

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:36.300
Yet in the mirror's crowd,
each snapshot still points one way.

00:27:36.670 --> 00:27:40.220
The mirror balances the
crowd, not each snapshot.

00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:45.060
Its card goes on the crowd
shelf: built in, exactly.

00:27:46.103 --> 00:27:48.743
Here is that tilt, setting by setting.

00:27:49.143 --> 00:27:52.586
Below the threshold,
runs often split unevenly:

00:27:52.666 --> 00:27:57.493
at zero point eight six, only
five of sixteen runs point up.

00:27:58.043 --> 00:28:03.193
The plain machine copies the imbalance,
and spills it onto unseen settings.

00:28:03.933 --> 00:28:07.443
Augmentation sits on the noise
floor at its training settings.

00:28:07.713 --> 00:28:11.933
Between them, it drifts:
the network fills the gaps freely.

00:28:12.343 --> 00:28:15.263
The built-in mirror stays
on the floor everywhere.

00:28:15.583 --> 00:28:19.063
Flipped copies teach the mirror
only where there are data.

00:28:20.103 --> 00:28:22.283
Can the energy budget catch the tilt?

00:28:22.733 --> 00:28:26.343
No. Each whirlpool
balances its own budget.

00:28:26.523 --> 00:28:29.853
So a mix of the two passes,
in any proportion.

00:28:30.263 --> 00:28:35.183
On a check of crowd laws, the most
lopsided machine even edges ahead.

00:28:35.533 --> 00:28:37.753
The mirror needs its own tool.

00:28:38.803 --> 00:28:40.903
Remember the budget of the shoebox.

00:28:41.083 --> 00:28:45.183
Snapshot by snapshot,
energy rises or falls.

00:28:45.483 --> 00:28:50.773
Only the crowd balances, on average,
like a bank account over the years.

00:28:51.513 --> 00:28:54.423
Now, a tempting fix for the machine.

00:28:54.643 --> 00:29:00.643
Force every generated snapshot to balance
its budget exactly. Better, or worse?

00:29:05.113 --> 00:29:06.183
Worse.

00:29:06.263 --> 00:29:10.056
Push each snapshot until
its energy stops changing:

00:29:10.136 --> 00:29:13.883
the wide pile collapses
into a spike at zero.

00:29:14.123 --> 00:29:17.393
A ledger where every single day balances.

00:29:17.683 --> 00:29:19.883
No real account looks like that.

00:29:20.623 --> 00:29:24.203
The distribution distance
to the truth grows ninefold.

00:29:24.783 --> 00:29:28.139
A short proof says why:
even a perfect generator,

00:29:28.219 --> 00:29:31.353
forced this way, cannot match the truth.

00:29:31.733 --> 00:29:34.153
So the budget goes on the crowd shelf.

00:29:34.553 --> 00:29:39.163
On the next test bench, a cruder
score is fooled by such a spike.

00:29:40.203 --> 00:29:43.243
Impose the budget on the
crowd, during training.

00:29:43.683 --> 00:29:47.643
Draw a batch of snapshots at
any Gamma, even without data.

00:29:48.083 --> 00:29:51.954
Average their energy change
and square it: this penalty,

00:29:52.034 --> 00:29:56.173
the crowd loss, vanishes
only when the crowd balances.

00:29:56.913 --> 00:29:58.113
But there is a trap.

00:29:58.553 --> 00:30:02.146
Even for a balanced crowd,
a batch's average wobbles;

00:30:02.226 --> 00:30:04.913
its square grows with the scatter.

00:30:05.123 --> 00:30:07.654
So training also squeezes the scatter,

00:30:07.734 --> 00:30:11.913
nudging every snapshot toward
balance: toward the spike.

00:30:12.533 --> 00:30:15.003
It showed up while
building this experiment.

00:30:15.743 --> 00:30:18.836
The fix: split the batch
into two independent

00:30:18.916 --> 00:30:21.713
juries and multiply their averages.

00:30:22.133 --> 00:30:27.233
On average, the scatter drops out,
leaving only the crowd's imbalance.

00:30:27.613 --> 00:30:31.143
Does it pay off? The next tests will tell.

00:30:32.470 --> 00:30:35.479
In three dimensions,
which budgets should generated

00:30:35.559 --> 00:30:39.280
fields meet on average,
and must their spread match too?

00:30:44.537 --> 00:30:48.497
New test bench: whole
fields, not three dials.

00:30:48.947 --> 00:30:53.167
A flat square of conducting fluid,
its edges wrapping around.

00:30:53.517 --> 00:30:56.516
In blue, the swirl of
the flow; in vermillion,

00:30:56.596 --> 00:30:59.537
the electric currents behind the field.

00:31:00.277 --> 00:31:04.027
One knob, eta: how fast the field smudges.

00:31:04.237 --> 00:31:07.217
But a flat world cannot sustain a dynamo.

00:31:07.427 --> 00:31:10.541
Here a random forcing
keeps the field alive:

00:31:10.621 --> 00:31:13.927
a training ground for laws, not a dynamo.

00:31:14.970 --> 00:31:19.330
Every training field passes the
box test: it is divergence-free.

00:31:19.760 --> 00:31:21.890
Train a plain generator on them.

00:31:22.630 --> 00:31:25.010
Will its fields be divergence-free?

00:31:28.750 --> 00:31:29.670
No.

00:31:29.750 --> 00:31:32.830
Now lines appear and
vanish inside the box.

00:31:33.150 --> 00:31:35.929
Relative to the field's own variations,

00:31:36.009 --> 00:31:40.810
the divergence is zero point
four one: a defect of order one.

00:31:41.550 --> 00:31:44.784
A soft penalty only lowers
it to zero point three

00:31:44.864 --> 00:31:48.710
four for the flow, zero
point one six for the field.

00:31:48.970 --> 00:31:53.060
And the magnetic energy comes out
two point seven times too high.

00:31:53.460 --> 00:31:56.800
This law belongs on the
shelf for every snapshot.

00:31:57.837 --> 00:32:00.724
First fix: draw a hill map, not arrows,

00:32:00.804 --> 00:32:04.557
and let the flow run
along its contour lines.

00:32:05.017 --> 00:32:07.580
Where the lines crowd, it runs faster,

00:32:07.660 --> 00:32:11.677
so nothing piles up or leaks,
whatever the landscape.

00:32:12.257 --> 00:32:16.197
The box agrees: lines in, lines out.

00:32:16.937 --> 00:32:21.008
So the machine draws two hill maps,
for the flow and the field:

00:32:21.088 --> 00:32:25.437
zero divergence by construction,
however badly it is trained.

00:32:26.177 --> 00:32:32.177
Second fix, for any field: split it into
swirl plus slope, and delete the slope.

00:32:33.237 --> 00:32:38.647
Wave by wave, remove the part of each
arrow along the wave's own direction.

00:32:39.387 --> 00:32:44.257
The gauges fall to rounding error,
down to ten to the minus seventeen.

00:32:44.487 --> 00:32:48.417
Measured, though, with the very
operator used to enforce it.

00:32:49.470 --> 00:32:54.400
Back to the plain generator:
why does it miss, trained on clean data?

00:32:55.140 --> 00:32:57.750
Follow the part of a
field breaking the law.

00:32:58.060 --> 00:33:01.853
To bring it exactly to zero,
the wind must act as a spring

00:33:01.933 --> 00:33:06.270
that turns infinitely stiff as
the transport clock runs out.

00:33:07.010 --> 00:33:12.390
A bounded network cannot, and a sampler
with finite steps cannot follow either.

00:33:12.820 --> 00:33:17.770
A gap of order one remains:
almost is not exactly.

00:33:18.803 --> 00:33:24.203
Now the crowd law: on average,
energy in equals energy out.

00:33:24.583 --> 00:33:28.773
Three ways to impose it,
at settings of eta never seen.

00:33:29.513 --> 00:33:34.063
One: during training, the crowd
loss, with its two juries.

00:33:34.333 --> 00:33:36.540
Against a control run of equal length,

00:33:36.620 --> 00:33:41.283
the distribution distance drops from
two point five to zero point seven.

00:33:41.533 --> 00:33:43.893
The control itself gains nothing.

00:33:44.633 --> 00:33:48.873
Two: after training,
reweight the generated crowd.

00:33:49.283 --> 00:33:52.403
Where the model is worst,
a poll of five hundred

00:33:52.483 --> 00:33:56.013
shrinks to one person,
and the crowd gets worse.

00:33:56.753 --> 00:34:00.703
Three: rescale every
snapshot onto the budget.

00:34:01.143 --> 00:34:04.281
Each snapshot now dissipates
the same: a spike,

00:34:04.361 --> 00:34:07.293
under a thousandth of the true spread.

00:34:07.613 --> 00:34:13.313
Yet the same distribution distance,
built on spectra, improves: it is fooled.

00:34:13.683 --> 00:34:18.783
Only the spread gives the spike away.
The crowd loss shows no spike.

00:34:19.523 --> 00:34:22.549
One training seed each,
interpolation only,

00:34:22.629 --> 00:34:25.703
every model far above the noise floor.

00:34:26.737 --> 00:34:29.147
Back to the two shelves, now full.

00:34:29.537 --> 00:34:33.303
For every snapshot: zero
divergence. Build it in,

00:34:33.383 --> 00:34:38.187
or project, and it holds exactly;
a penalty only roughly.

00:34:38.927 --> 00:34:42.615
For the crowd: the energy budget.
Impose it on the crowd

00:34:42.695 --> 00:34:47.326
during training. Reweighting afterwards
fails where the model is poor;

00:34:47.406 --> 00:34:50.697
forcing it onto each
snapshot makes a spike.

00:34:51.437 --> 00:34:56.142
And the mirror? Each snapshot is
lopsided, so it is a crowd law,

00:34:56.222 --> 00:34:59.917
yet a network can carry
it exactly, built in.

00:35:00.657 --> 00:35:05.397
One rule sorts them all:
enforce each law where it is true.

00:35:08.337 --> 00:35:12.077
Put the pieces together:
the machine this study proposes.

00:35:12.317 --> 00:35:17.337
A wind carries noise to a symmetric
crowd of codes, mirror built in.

00:35:17.697 --> 00:35:23.327
A decoder turns each code into hill maps,
so every field is divergence-free.

00:35:23.617 --> 00:35:26.407
And the energy budget
is imposed on the crowd.

00:35:27.147 --> 00:35:31.250
It has not been built as a whole:
the experiments tested pieces,

00:35:31.330 --> 00:35:36.757
on three dials and a flat sheet, with
small networks on an ordinary processor.

00:35:37.497 --> 00:35:43.027
One question remains: from snapshots,
how well can we answer how often?

00:35:44.170 --> 00:35:48.036
In the partners' solver snapshots,
which laws hold exactly,

00:35:48.116 --> 00:35:50.720
and under which discrete operator?

00:35:54.770 --> 00:35:58.200
An aside: the five statements
behind the pictures.

00:35:58.450 --> 00:36:02.540
Each is elementary; what
matters is its exact scope.

00:36:03.280 --> 00:36:05.500
The first is almost too simple.

00:36:05.850 --> 00:36:11.780
Suppose every field a decoder can draw
obeys a linear law, like zero divergence.

00:36:12.010 --> 00:36:17.240
Then every sample obeys it, whatever the
crowd of codes, however badly trained.

00:36:17.980 --> 00:36:19.850
The proof is one line.

00:36:20.060 --> 00:36:23.815
The work is in the decoder:
hill maps in two dimensions,

00:36:23.895 --> 00:36:27.650
a vector potential in three,
with its gauge fixed.

00:36:28.703 --> 00:36:31.163
Second: the spring, in full.

00:36:31.763 --> 00:36:36.783
If the data lie in a flat subspace,
the ideal wind splits in two.

00:36:37.153 --> 00:36:40.103
Along it, a part a network can learn.

00:36:40.383 --> 00:36:46.383
Across it, a pull back: the stray part,
divided by the time left on the clock.

00:36:47.293 --> 00:36:51.813
Followed exactly, that pull lands
every sample in the subspace.

00:36:52.453 --> 00:36:55.090
Trained perfectly, the plain generator,

00:36:55.170 --> 00:36:59.053
both projections and hill
maps give the same crowd.

00:36:59.793 --> 00:37:03.583
Cap the pull, and an
order-one stray part survives.

00:37:04.093 --> 00:37:09.923
Now add a small, bounded error to the
exact pull. Does a stray part survive?

00:37:13.163 --> 00:37:15.573
No. The pull erases it.

00:37:15.943 --> 00:37:18.968
So the plain generator's
zero point four one

00:37:19.048 --> 00:37:22.413
reads as a missing pull,
not a small error.

00:37:23.153 --> 00:37:26.963
And the projected flow's tiny
residue? Single precision.

00:37:28.003 --> 00:37:33.893
Third: penalties. A common one tests the
model's guess of where its path will end.

00:37:34.323 --> 00:37:38.863
With the ideal wind, that guess
averages the data that could lie ahead.

00:37:39.603 --> 00:37:43.302
For a linear law, an average
of lawful fields is lawful:

00:37:43.382 --> 00:37:47.423
the penalty vanishes at the
optimum, and moves nothing.

00:37:47.893 --> 00:37:52.723
Off the optimum, in a real network,
it holds the law only roughly.

00:37:53.463 --> 00:37:59.433
For a curved law, that fails: two points
on a circle average to a point inside.

00:37:59.753 --> 00:38:04.773
This is the Jensen gap: now the
penalty biases the optimum itself.

00:38:05.803 --> 00:38:08.813
Fourth: the mirror, as a theorem.

00:38:09.303 --> 00:38:14.723
Make the wind equivariant: mirror
a point, and its arrow mirrors too.

00:38:15.103 --> 00:38:18.143
Start from noise that looks
the same in the mirror.

00:38:18.883 --> 00:38:21.293
Then every path has a mirror twin.

00:38:21.373 --> 00:38:24.619
So the generated crowd is
exactly symmetric at every

00:38:24.699 --> 00:38:28.163
knob setting, even with
lopsided or missing data.

00:38:28.903 --> 00:38:32.112
If every true crowd is
symmetric, averaging a wind

00:38:32.192 --> 00:38:35.613
with its mirror never
raises the expected loss.

00:38:35.913 --> 00:38:39.393
The cost: two network
passes instead of one.

00:38:40.133 --> 00:38:45.653
In the VKS cylinder, a half-turn swapping
the two impellers is a second symmetry.

00:38:46.113 --> 00:38:49.002
If their speeds differ,
it also flips the sign

00:38:49.082 --> 00:38:52.123
of that difference, the forcing asymmetry.

00:38:52.543 --> 00:38:55.953
The same proof covers it, untested so far.

00:38:57.003 --> 00:38:59.829
Last, crowd laws. The equations give any

00:38:59.909 --> 00:39:03.363
quantity a rate of
change at each snapshot.

00:39:03.753 --> 00:39:07.554
If the crowd is stationary,
that rate averages to zero,

00:39:07.634 --> 00:39:11.493
for every quantity;
the energy budget is one case.

00:39:12.233 --> 00:39:16.013
The proof: let every
snapshot run for a moment.

00:39:16.653 --> 00:39:22.173
The crowd looks the same, so no
average moves: the mean rate is zero.

00:39:22.913 --> 00:39:26.423
Necessary, not sufficient:
a crowd parked on an

00:39:26.503 --> 00:39:30.193
unstable fixed point
passes every such test.

00:39:30.303 --> 00:39:32.103
So does an unstable loop.

00:39:32.613 --> 00:39:35.873
The fit to data must
therefore stay in the loss.

00:39:37.003 --> 00:39:40.032
Can we simulate only one
sign of forcing asymmetry,

00:39:40.112 --> 00:39:43.063
and let the impeller
swap supply the other?

00:39:45.903 --> 00:39:49.503
Earlier, a distribution
distance meant moving sand.

00:39:49.883 --> 00:39:53.613
The experiments mainly use
a cousin, built from pairs.

00:39:53.863 --> 00:39:56.073
Each dot is a snapshot.

00:39:56.813 --> 00:40:00.663
Average every distance across
the two crowds, and double it.

00:40:01.053 --> 00:40:04.123
Subtract the average
distance within each crowd.

00:40:04.413 --> 00:40:06.673
Zero means the same law.

00:40:08.903 --> 00:40:10.853
Reweighting, up close.

00:40:11.243 --> 00:40:14.103
The model's crowd misses
its target average.

00:40:14.483 --> 00:40:19.513
Leave every snapshot in place;
change only how much each counts.

00:40:20.253 --> 00:40:22.093
Many weightings hit the target.

00:40:22.443 --> 00:40:27.503
The least distorting one is an exponential
tilt, known as the I-projection.

00:40:28.243 --> 00:40:33.483
Picture crowds as points: those with
the right average lie on a line.

00:40:33.743 --> 00:40:36.823
The tilted crowd is the
foot of the perpendicular.

00:40:37.253 --> 00:40:42.633
Any other fix pays the tilt's cost
plus an extra, as in Pythagoras.

00:40:42.943 --> 00:40:48.653
And nothing moves: the tilted crowd
lives only where the model's crowd was.

00:40:49.703 --> 00:40:52.333
How much is left of a reweighted crowd?

00:40:52.453 --> 00:40:54.613
Its effective sample size says.

00:40:54.973 --> 00:40:57.383
Equal weights: the whole crowd.

00:40:57.703 --> 00:41:00.553
One giant weight: one person.

00:41:01.293 --> 00:41:06.693
On the flat sheet, at the smallest test
eta, the poll keeps ninety-nine percent.

00:41:07.103 --> 00:41:12.353
At the three largest, it bottoms out:
one snapshot in five hundred.

00:41:13.093 --> 00:41:16.405
Rank the values by how far
the model's budget was off:

00:41:16.485 --> 00:41:19.453
the poll shrinks in nearly the same order.

00:41:19.993 --> 00:41:22.993
Where the poll survives,
little needed fixing.

00:41:23.353 --> 00:41:26.313
Where much needed fixing, it collapses.

00:41:27.053 --> 00:41:30.792
On the three dials, too,
reweighting improved no crowd:

00:41:30.872 --> 00:41:34.813
one snapshot weighed
thirty-three times the average.

00:41:35.870 --> 00:41:38.807
Picture a model that never
visits the bridge between

00:41:38.887 --> 00:41:42.060
the two polarities,
where every crossing happens.

00:41:42.800 --> 00:41:45.370
Can reweighting bring
back the crossing rate?

00:41:48.610 --> 00:41:52.034
No. Rice reads the rate inside the strip,

00:41:52.114 --> 00:41:57.230
and the strip is empty. Zero,
times any weight, is zero.

00:41:57.690 --> 00:42:02.130
Push harder, and the weight piles
onto a few snapshots at the edge.

00:42:02.870 --> 00:42:06.150
Reweighting cannot visit
places the model never went.

00:42:06.540 --> 00:42:11.990
The PhD plan's risk table agrees:
reweighting alone is no fallback.

00:42:13.037 --> 00:42:15.027
One test eta at a time.

00:42:15.487 --> 00:42:18.937
At the largest, the base
model's snapshots dissipate,

00:42:19.017 --> 00:42:22.077
on average, over twice the energy fed in.

00:42:22.567 --> 00:42:25.303
The crowd loss cuts the
distribution distance

00:42:25.383 --> 00:42:28.217
from seven point seven to two point two.

00:42:28.957 --> 00:42:32.557
At the smallest, already good: no change.

00:42:33.007 --> 00:42:34.937
The control run stays put.

00:42:35.677 --> 00:42:37.995
Same values where the poll collapsed:

00:42:38.075 --> 00:42:41.257
training moved the crowd;
weights could not.

00:42:41.577 --> 00:42:43.007
One seed, though.

00:42:44.137 --> 00:42:46.871
If the crowd loss fails,
what is the fallback,

00:42:46.951 --> 00:42:50.217
since reweighting fails
where the model is poor?

00:42:55.270 --> 00:42:59.400
The toll counter is back:
every car is now generated.

00:42:59.780 --> 00:43:02.836
They come from the machine
with the built-in mirror:

00:43:02.916 --> 00:43:05.007
twenty thousand at each of forty-one

00:43:05.087 --> 00:43:08.010
settings of Gamma,
most unseen in training.

00:43:08.490 --> 00:43:12.580
Rice, with the equations,
turns them into a crossing rate.

00:43:13.320 --> 00:43:16.438
Against the counted truth,
far from the threshold,

00:43:16.518 --> 00:43:19.470
the cumulative error is fourteen percent.

00:43:19.910 --> 00:43:23.451
Setting by setting, the miss
averages seventeen percent,

00:43:23.531 --> 00:43:26.900
and reaches sixty-five
percent at two settings.

00:43:27.470 --> 00:43:31.450
Near the threshold, the error
climbs to forty-two percent.

00:43:32.190 --> 00:43:34.640
One model, one training seed.

00:43:34.900 --> 00:43:38.960
Rice needs the equations,
and gives a rate, not waiting times.

00:43:39.550 --> 00:43:44.080
So, the study's third question:
can a machine tell us how often?

00:43:44.430 --> 00:43:50.430
For crossings on three dials: far from the
threshold, roughly yes; near it, not yet.

00:43:52.570 --> 00:43:57.480
Zoom in. The truth is silent
up to zero point eight six.

00:43:57.840 --> 00:44:00.600
But the machine changes
smoothly with Gamma.

00:44:01.340 --> 00:44:05.410
Will its crossing rate switch
on early, late, or on time?

00:44:09.150 --> 00:44:12.070
Early and late, but never on time.

00:44:12.560 --> 00:44:15.478
Below the threshold,
it leaks false crossings:

00:44:15.558 --> 00:44:18.300
up to a tenth of the rate just above.

00:44:18.740 --> 00:44:24.480
At zero point eight six five, it is late:
less than a sixth of the counted rate.

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:28.213
Just above, it overshoots:
five crossings per

00:44:28.293 --> 00:44:31.990
thousand time units,
against three point four.

00:44:33.037 --> 00:44:36.463
Why? Picture the machine as a stiff sheet:

00:44:36.543 --> 00:44:40.797
as Gamma turns, its crowd
changes only so fast.

00:44:41.117 --> 00:44:47.117
The truth climbs a steep ramp: just above
zero point eight six, the corridor opens.

00:44:48.587 --> 00:44:52.587
The sheet rounds the corner:
too early, then too low.

00:44:52.957 --> 00:44:58.737
The data show both: early strays in the
corridor, then too few at the threshold.

00:44:59.477 --> 00:45:05.047
A theorem: at a true jump, a smooth
machine misses by at least half of it.

00:45:05.457 --> 00:45:09.857
Unless it is very supple,
or told which state the system is in.

00:45:10.597 --> 00:45:16.597
Here, a ramp, not a jump, and one seed:
consistent with the theorem, not proof.

00:45:17.897 --> 00:45:20.517
Nor does the theorem
explain the overshoot.

00:45:21.970 --> 00:45:25.840
The crowd loss, on three
dials, at unseen settings.

00:45:26.310 --> 00:45:29.770
On the good machine,
it halves the crowd-law residuals,

00:45:29.850 --> 00:45:32.240
even for laws it never enforced.

00:45:32.730 --> 00:45:38.540
But the spread of energy changes shrinks
by a quarter: a faint echo of the spike.

00:45:39.280 --> 00:45:42.608
On a deliberately biased
machine, it fails:

00:45:42.688 --> 00:45:46.030
one run diverged, the other got worse.

00:45:46.450 --> 00:45:48.862
On the crossing rate, with one seed:

00:45:48.942 --> 00:45:53.010
slightly better near the
threshold, worse far from it.

00:45:54.437 --> 00:45:57.437
Now the strictest judge: the noise floor.

00:45:57.657 --> 00:46:00.644
On three dials, the best
machine sits at about

00:46:00.724 --> 00:46:03.987
twice its floor;
but three numbers are easy.

00:46:04.277 --> 00:46:08.290
On the flat sheet of plasma,
every model's spectra miss

00:46:08.370 --> 00:46:12.867
far more than two data samples
differ: at best, sixfold.

00:46:13.607 --> 00:46:19.607
Two seeds on the dials, one on the sheet;
the PhD plan asks for at least three.

00:46:20.167 --> 00:46:23.007
Every test sits between trained settings.

00:46:24.437 --> 00:46:28.367
The scoreboard: eight mechanisms,
each with a verdict.

00:46:28.797 --> 00:46:32.399
For every snapshot:
hill maps and projection,

00:46:32.479 --> 00:46:35.427
exact; the penalty, approximate.

00:46:35.967 --> 00:46:40.591
For the crowd: the mirror,
exact; the crowd loss,

00:46:40.671 --> 00:46:44.937
useful but not clean; the rest, failures.

00:46:45.677 --> 00:46:47.837
Back to the study's three questions.

00:46:48.167 --> 00:46:51.591
One: between the dots,
the shapes come out right,

00:46:51.671 --> 00:46:54.647
but no model reaches its noise floor.

00:46:55.157 --> 00:47:00.967
Two: each law where it holds,
exactly yes, on average partly.

00:47:01.517 --> 00:47:07.477
Three, how often: roughly right far
from the threshold, poor near it.

00:47:08.217 --> 00:47:09.417
Three lessons.

00:47:09.837 --> 00:47:12.427
Exact laws: build them in.

00:47:12.837 --> 00:47:17.737
Crowd laws: impose them on the
crowd, never on each snapshot.

00:47:18.167 --> 00:47:22.697
And judge every result against the
noise floor, with enough seeds.

00:47:24.537 --> 00:47:27.762
Which settings should get runs
long enough to count reversals,

00:47:27.842 --> 00:47:30.037
so a predicted rate can be checked?

00:47:32.470 --> 00:47:34.740
How wide should Rice's strip be?

00:47:35.190 --> 00:47:40.130
Widen it: the estimate blurs,
its bias growing like the width squared.

00:47:40.460 --> 00:47:45.210
Narrow it: the estimate jitters,
as fewer snapshots land inside.

00:47:45.640 --> 00:47:50.590
The three-dial experiment used
snapshots within zero point two of zero.

00:47:51.330 --> 00:47:55.090
With endless snapshots,
why not shrink it to nothing?

00:47:55.500 --> 00:48:00.546
The exact formula assumes a smooth
cloud. A cosine has only a thin ring,

00:48:00.626 --> 00:48:04.730
yet the formula still finds
two crossings per period.

00:48:05.470 --> 00:48:09.990
Chaos may lack a smooth cloud too,
so nothing guarantees the limit:

00:48:10.070 --> 00:48:15.480
on the three dials, the only test so
far, the answer depends on the strip.

00:48:16.737 --> 00:48:20.292
To count reversals,
not crossings, raise the bar:

00:48:20.372 --> 00:48:25.747
a reversal must run from above some
level to below its mirror image.

00:48:25.967 --> 00:48:27.723
So the level's crossing rate,

00:48:27.803 --> 00:48:31.677
which Rice reads from snapshots,
caps the reversal rate.

00:48:32.417 --> 00:48:34.947
Wobbles across the level loosen the cap.

00:48:35.487 --> 00:48:39.348
The conjecture: if a flip
lasts far longer than a wobble,

00:48:39.428 --> 00:48:43.007
the cap is nearly exact
on a plateau of levels.

00:48:43.747 --> 00:48:49.375
The bet, written before the data, from the
PhD plan: on the staircase's first step,

00:48:49.455 --> 00:48:54.737
the cap comes within thirty percent
of the counted rate on that plateau.

00:48:56.277 --> 00:49:01.507
Confidence: medium; the cap
is proved, the plateau is not.

00:49:01.987 --> 00:49:05.597
Ask first: how wide must that plateau be?

00:49:06.837 --> 00:49:09.217
One more limit, a proved one.

00:49:09.497 --> 00:49:13.918
These two smooth random
signals share one snapshot law,

00:49:13.998 --> 00:49:17.437
value and speed alike: same Rice rate.

00:49:17.817 --> 00:49:22.837
Yet one crosses zero like a metronome,
the other at uneven gaps.

00:49:23.577 --> 00:49:28.305
On three dials, Gissinger found a
shape in the waits between flips:

00:49:28.385 --> 00:49:33.667
a comb of short, quantized waits,
then a memoryless exponential tail.

00:49:34.407 --> 00:49:38.037
No shoebox holds that shape;
it takes the movie.

00:49:38.497 --> 00:49:40.649
The open question: can short runs,

00:49:40.729 --> 00:49:45.327
launched from generated states in
the corridor, supply the waits?

00:49:46.370 --> 00:49:49.260
The staircase's first
step gets a second round.

00:49:49.490 --> 00:49:51.520
Three seeds, not two.

00:49:51.740 --> 00:49:55.720
A second model, distilled
from VKS simulations.

00:49:56.030 --> 00:49:59.690
And the impeller-swap mirror,
which also flips Gamma.

00:50:00.430 --> 00:50:06.430
Four lamps must light: lopsidedness,
unenforced laws, Rice, the level bound.

00:50:09.020 --> 00:50:13.704
The bet: unenforced residuals
halve again, roughly. Last time,

00:50:13.784 --> 00:50:19.340
halving meant one point nine eight,
against a bar of two: a toss-up.

00:50:19.770 --> 00:50:21.630
Confidence: medium.

00:50:22.080 --> 00:50:25.904
One catch: a perfectly
symmetric model lands above the

00:50:25.984 --> 00:50:30.440
noise floor nearly half the time;
the floor is its average.

00:50:30.520 --> 00:50:34.810
Ask first: should the first
lamp use a statistical test?

00:50:36.137 --> 00:50:40.695
Beyond how often: for VKS,
should we also target waiting times,

00:50:40.775 --> 00:50:44.417
with short solver runs
from generated states?

00:50:49.470 --> 00:50:52.784
So far, every test ran
on toy models. Before

00:50:52.864 --> 00:50:56.510
real dynamos, back to
the map of the field.

00:50:56.710 --> 00:51:01.760
Zoom into its thin data-driven
lane: it opens into four tracks.

00:51:02.500 --> 00:51:07.654
A laboratory dynamo sets every
track four tests: three dimensions,

00:51:07.734 --> 00:51:13.734
walls with iron and a vacuum, a tiny
magnetic Prandtl number, and rare flips.

00:51:15.603 --> 00:51:21.033
First track: fast surrogates,
networks trained to imitate a solver.

00:51:21.353 --> 00:51:26.703
Some carry the equations in their loss;
others learn maps between whole fields.

00:51:27.123 --> 00:51:32.153
For conducting fluids, they now reach
flat, two-dimensional turbulence.

00:51:32.273 --> 00:51:35.488
For the Earth, recall the
twenty twenty-two study:

00:51:35.568 --> 00:51:39.523
the methods it tried could
not reliably foresee a flip.

00:51:40.263 --> 00:51:44.036
Their weak spot: trained to
minimize a mean-square error,

00:51:44.116 --> 00:51:47.353
ensembles of them
regress to the mean flow.

00:51:47.593 --> 00:51:51.213
A cloud with two polarities
collapses to its middle.

00:51:52.270 --> 00:51:54.347
Second track: generative models,

00:51:54.427 --> 00:51:58.060
which learn the whole cloud,
not just its middle.

00:51:58.530 --> 00:52:01.503
Adversarial networks
first, then diffusion,

00:52:01.583 --> 00:52:05.180
then flow matching,
the family the study used.

00:52:05.630 --> 00:52:09.892
They now dream three-dimensional
turbulence, global weather ensembles,

00:52:09.972 --> 00:52:13.280
and steady turbulent
states for chosen settings.

00:52:14.020 --> 00:52:17.580
Trained on dynamo
simulations? We found none.

00:52:19.437 --> 00:52:24.537
Third track: making samples
obey the laws. Four families.

00:52:24.927 --> 00:52:29.192
Hard: build the law into the network,
say a potential whose curl

00:52:29.272 --> 00:52:33.607
has no divergence, or project
each sample while drawing it.

00:52:33.987 --> 00:52:38.907
Soft: a penalty in the loss,
obeyed only approximately.

00:52:39.137 --> 00:52:42.112
On the distribution: constrain the crowd,

00:52:42.192 --> 00:52:46.917
by its covariance, or by the
equation a steady cloud obeys.

00:52:47.357 --> 00:52:52.197
And symmetry: an equivariant
flow gives a symmetric cloud.

00:52:53.337 --> 00:52:57.222
A twenty twenty-six preprint
compared ways to impose zero

00:52:57.302 --> 00:53:01.457
divergence on flat flows:
no single strategy dominates.

00:53:01.897 --> 00:53:06.952
In three dimensions, we found two
exactly divergence-free generators,

00:53:07.032 --> 00:53:09.928
both preprints: periodic-box turbulence,

00:53:10.008 --> 00:53:13.687
and a current-free field
in the Sun's corona.

00:53:13.777 --> 00:53:18.497
None handles walls, a vacuum
outside and iron inside.

00:53:19.537 --> 00:53:24.777
Fourth track: data assimilation,
merging a model with a few measurements.

00:53:25.117 --> 00:53:30.487
Learned priors came in through compressed
latent spaces, then diffusion models.

00:53:30.657 --> 00:53:36.047
And a library now plugs deep networks into
Kalman filters and variational methods.

00:53:36.787 --> 00:53:40.737
We found no diffusion prior used
this way for conducting fluids.

00:53:41.117 --> 00:53:43.712
Near a flip, the answer
has two polarities,

00:53:43.792 --> 00:53:47.917
and a prior that ignores the
mirror could bias it toward one.

00:53:48.970 --> 00:53:52.990
Now the rings: papers
co-signed by the supervisors.

00:53:53.710 --> 00:53:59.148
Dynamo side: Taylor–Green dynamos pushed
to small magnetic Prandtl numbers,

00:53:59.228 --> 00:54:04.340
and the VKS tank with its iron
impellers, simulated in two codes.

00:54:04.450 --> 00:54:08.537
In twenty twenty-six, both
codes produced VKS reversals,

00:54:08.617 --> 00:54:12.890
and a four-dial model
reproduces their main features.

00:54:13.660 --> 00:54:17.486
Learning side: autoencoder
latent spaces read through

00:54:17.566 --> 00:54:21.360
classical flow modes,
von Kármán flow included.

00:54:21.650 --> 00:54:24.437
And assimilation and
diffusion models built

00:54:24.517 --> 00:54:27.780
on deep networks,
outside conducting fluids.

00:54:28.920 --> 00:54:31.964
Three ingredients are in hand.
But in what we found,

00:54:32.044 --> 00:54:36.760
none of the four has published a
generative model trained on dynamo data.

00:54:37.803 --> 00:54:40.491
Where the generative and
constraint tracks cross

00:54:40.571 --> 00:54:43.323
the dynamo tracks, we found the map empty.

00:54:43.803 --> 00:54:47.179
An independent thesis
proposal aims at that spot:

00:54:47.259 --> 00:54:50.763
five gaps, each beside
its nearest neighbour.

00:54:51.503 --> 00:54:54.593
One: a generator of dynamo fields.

00:54:55.183 --> 00:54:58.387
Two: velocity and magnetic
field with exactly

00:54:58.467 --> 00:55:03.103
zero divergence, in a walled
cylinder with iron and vacuum.

00:55:03.933 --> 00:55:07.388
Three: energy budgets
imposed on the distribution,

00:55:07.468 --> 00:55:11.403
with no target value,
even where there are no data.

00:55:11.523 --> 00:55:16.113
Four: the flip rate read from
snapshots with Rice's formula.

00:55:16.583 --> 00:55:20.953
Five: one generator that
crosses the dynamo threshold.

00:55:22.093 --> 00:55:27.381
The study tested pieces on toy cases:
exact potentials on a flat sheet,

00:55:27.461 --> 00:55:32.403
a budget imposed without data,
Rice's formula on three dials.

00:55:33.437 --> 00:55:37.723
The field moves fast: a twenty twenty-six
conference paper claims to be the

00:55:37.803 --> 00:55:42.557
first to recognize a trade-off between
physical and distributional accuracy.

00:55:42.977 --> 00:55:46.040
The study's lesson,
never force a crowd law

00:55:46.120 --> 00:55:49.667
on each snapshot,
must be placed against it.

00:55:50.407 --> 00:55:53.552
Not claimed: waiting times between flips,

00:55:53.632 --> 00:55:59.632
sodium's tiny magnetic Prandtl number,
the Earth's core, real laboratory data.

00:56:00.557 --> 00:56:03.754
Each empty spot is a
search result, not a proof,

00:56:03.834 --> 00:56:06.827
to be checked again before every paper.

00:56:08.370 --> 00:56:11.865
First VKS data: should
the generator train on the

00:56:11.945 --> 00:56:15.850
LATU periodic grid,
or the SFEMaNS cylinder mesh?

00:56:20.903 --> 00:56:22.383
Back to the staircase.

00:56:22.753 --> 00:56:26.097
The first two steps are
lit by small experiments:

00:56:26.177 --> 00:56:28.813
three numbers, then a flat sheet.

00:56:29.283 --> 00:56:33.353
The third, the twisting
box, has only a preview.

00:56:34.093 --> 00:56:36.710
From there up, every step is a bet,

00:56:36.790 --> 00:56:42.283
written down before the data exist,
in an independent PhD proposal.

00:56:43.337 --> 00:56:46.807
To climb, the study first
built a factory for the data.

00:56:47.087 --> 00:56:49.347
A recipe card sets the knobs.

00:56:49.557 --> 00:56:54.237
Ovens run one simulation per setting,
on a laptop or a cluster.

00:56:54.747 --> 00:56:57.593
Each result is sealed in a labelled jar:

00:56:57.673 --> 00:57:01.367
a unique number,
the code version, a checksum.

00:57:02.107 --> 00:57:06.875
A ledger records every jar, a menu
splits them for training and testing,

00:57:06.955 --> 00:57:10.517
the chef learns, and a
critic scores the result.

00:57:11.257 --> 00:57:14.532
It all runs end to end
on an ordinary processor,

00:57:14.612 --> 00:57:18.227
never yet on a graphics
card or a real cluster.

00:57:18.807 --> 00:57:23.417
A grey tag names the file
each empty frame awaits.

00:57:24.470 --> 00:57:27.659
First product off the
line: the flat sheet,

00:57:27.739 --> 00:57:31.110
eleven diffusivities, sixteen runs each.

00:57:31.380 --> 00:57:34.570
The bet, a check on the
data: at every setting,

00:57:34.650 --> 00:57:39.990
the average dissipation matches the
injected power, within five percent.

00:57:42.437 --> 00:57:46.271
Open one oven: the solver
keeps flow and field as waves,

00:57:46.351 --> 00:57:48.987
where a curl is a multiplication.

00:57:49.367 --> 00:57:53.567
Products are easier on the grid:
each step hops over and back.

00:57:53.917 --> 00:57:59.097
It trims the outer third of the waves,
so products cannot fold back as fakes.

00:57:59.317 --> 00:58:02.666
A projection keeps both
fields divergence-free;

00:58:02.746 --> 00:58:05.337
the smudging is solved exactly.

00:58:06.077 --> 00:58:08.024
One step at sixty-four cubed:

00:58:08.104 --> 00:58:12.277
eight hundredths of a second,
on an ordinary processor.

00:58:12.617 --> 00:58:15.710
The memory peak, about
fifteen times the state,

00:58:15.790 --> 00:58:20.237
is an estimated six gigabytes
at two hundred fifty-six cubed.

00:58:21.270 --> 00:58:25.673
Open one jar: one simulation,
one file, laid out like The Well,

00:58:25.753 --> 00:58:29.000
a reference dataset for machine learning.

00:58:29.350 --> 00:58:32.401
Inside: the velocity
and the magnetic field,

00:58:32.481 --> 00:58:37.720
snapshot after snapshot, and time
series of energies and divergences.

00:58:38.020 --> 00:58:41.930
The file carries a unique
identifier and the code's commit.

00:58:42.670 --> 00:58:46.150
The catalog repeats that
identifier and adds a checksum.

00:58:46.280 --> 00:58:50.390
So an offline cluster can rebuild
its rows from the files alone.

00:58:50.570 --> 00:58:55.450
Checksummed, not sealed: a forced
rewrite updates the checksum.

00:58:56.503 --> 00:59:01.112
Follow one campaign down the line.
One command reads the recipe:

00:59:01.192 --> 00:59:04.233
the flat sheet, eleven diffusivities.

00:59:04.463 --> 00:59:07.348
The launcher opens one task per setting;

00:59:07.428 --> 00:59:12.353
each integrates sixteen runs
side by side and writes one file.

00:59:12.733 --> 00:59:18.253
Each task adds a ledger row:
its status, checksum and wall time.

00:59:18.993 --> 00:59:21.920
This campaign has not run yet. The bet:

00:59:22.000 --> 00:59:26.493
all eleven tasks finish,
each with a checksummed file.

00:59:26.983 --> 00:59:32.183
Confidence is high: small test
campaigns already finished, in seconds.

00:59:32.643 --> 00:59:37.193
The report's own data, from the same
solver, took about half an hour.

00:59:39.837 --> 00:59:43.212
A new instrument for the
critic: in the flat sheet,

00:59:43.292 --> 00:59:46.467
field lines are contours of one potential.

00:59:46.807 --> 00:59:50.637
Lines loop around O-points
and cross at X-points.

00:59:51.087 --> 00:59:55.037
On this periodic square,
which kind is more common?

00:59:58.277 --> 01:00:03.577
Neither: exactly as many of each,
a free check on any counter.

01:00:04.317 --> 01:00:09.037
The internship offer asks for
topology; the report computed none.

01:00:09.267 --> 01:00:13.917
A low-confidence bet: generated
fields match the simulated count.

01:00:14.177 --> 01:00:17.657
The plan lists the count,
but sets no pass mark.

01:00:19.603 --> 01:00:22.879
Which topology metric should
judge generated fields,

01:00:22.959 --> 01:00:26.193
and what gap in X-point
counts fails a model?

01:00:28.637 --> 01:00:32.367
Before trusting the solver,
test it on a known answer.

01:00:32.677 --> 01:00:37.297
The twisting flow offers one:
a field shaped exactly like it.

01:00:37.607 --> 01:00:42.797
Its lines follow the flow's own lines,
so carrying and stretching cancel.

01:00:43.087 --> 01:00:49.087
Only diffusion acts: its growth
rate is exactly minus one over Rm.

01:00:49.857 --> 01:00:52.992
So the measured rate, the slowest fade,

01:00:53.072 --> 01:00:56.947
can be no lower:
at Rm two, minus one half.

01:00:57.317 --> 01:01:00.931
The solver finds minus zero
point five zero zero one,

01:01:01.011 --> 01:01:04.617
on the bound to about
one part in ten thousand.

01:01:05.670 --> 01:01:08.696
Run long enough, and one field takes over:

01:01:08.776 --> 01:01:12.360
the slowest to fade,
or the fastest to grow.

01:01:12.810 --> 01:01:17.570
The surface wraps its strong
parts; threads trace its lines.

01:01:18.310 --> 01:01:20.610
At Rm eight, it fades.

01:01:20.930 --> 01:01:25.060
At twelve and sixteen,
inside the window, it grows.

01:01:25.450 --> 01:01:28.810
At twenty, just past it, it fades again.

01:01:29.370 --> 01:01:35.370
In these snapshots, at eight, sixteen and
twenty, it gathers in four slim tubes.

01:01:35.830 --> 01:01:38.870
They peak at points where
the flow stands still.

01:01:39.200 --> 01:01:41.490
At twelve, it spreads out.

01:01:42.230 --> 01:01:45.277
Published work describes
such cigar-shaped tubes,

01:01:45.357 --> 01:01:48.240
and symmetries the report did not check.

01:01:48.560 --> 01:01:54.150
One shape per setting: proposed first
targets for a three-dimensional generator.

01:01:55.403 --> 01:01:59.023
How precise are those edges?
Each run gets a long fit

01:01:59.103 --> 01:02:03.273
over its second half, a late
fit over its last quarter.

01:02:03.593 --> 01:02:09.323
A finer grid rechecks two settings.
At Rm twelve, all three agree.

01:02:10.063 --> 01:02:13.983
At seventeen and a half,
right on the edge: same sign?

01:02:17.223 --> 01:02:22.703
No: the long fit says growing;
the late fit and finer grid, fading.

01:02:23.013 --> 01:02:25.943
This edge is known to
about zero point one;

01:02:26.023 --> 01:02:29.363
the third, near twenty-seven,
to about one.

01:02:29.783 --> 01:02:35.733
A likely cause, still unchecked: the modes
oscillate, so the growth curve wobbles.

01:02:35.923 --> 01:02:39.083
The divergence, at least,
stays at rounding error.

01:02:39.253 --> 01:02:42.773
That unresolved sign is
where Bet one begins.

01:02:43.903 --> 01:02:46.578
Near the thresholds,
should we fit growth rates

01:02:46.658 --> 01:02:49.743
with an oscillation term,
or simply run longer?

01:02:51.970 --> 01:02:54.410
Bet one: the twisting box.

01:02:54.860 --> 01:02:59.075
At thirty-two cubed, the small
sweep matched the published windows,

01:02:59.155 --> 01:03:03.780
but near seventeen and a half the
sign of growth stayed unresolved.

01:03:04.520 --> 01:03:09.710
The campaign doubles the resolution and
steps Rm by whole numbers up to forty.

01:03:10.270 --> 01:03:13.328
The bet: growth still
positive at seventeen,

01:03:13.408 --> 01:03:17.109
already negative at eighteen,
with two fits agreeing:

01:03:17.189 --> 01:03:21.870
one over the kept part of the run,
one over its last quarter.

01:03:23.410 --> 01:03:27.136
Confidence: medium. At eighteen,
the small sweep's two

01:03:27.216 --> 01:03:31.140
fits already differed by
more than the rate itself.

01:03:31.610 --> 01:03:36.790
Worth asking before the run: add three
points between seventeen and eighteen?

01:03:38.037 --> 01:03:40.437
Bet two, the first true dynamo:

01:03:40.517 --> 01:03:44.607
a Taylor-Green flow of
counter-rotating eddies.

01:03:45.157 --> 01:03:47.677
Now the field pushes back on the flow.

01:03:47.957 --> 01:03:52.897
Above a threshold, a tiny seed
should grow, then level off.

01:03:53.637 --> 01:03:58.047
Twelve runs cross four Reynolds
numbers with three values of Pm.

01:03:58.647 --> 01:04:04.547
The bet: a threshold in Rm,
bracketed for at least two values of Pm.

01:04:06.087 --> 01:04:11.122
Confidence: medium. Published runs
define the Reynolds number differently,

01:04:11.202 --> 01:04:14.297
so the threshold may fall off this grid.

01:04:14.777 --> 01:04:19.447
Near threshold, a run may still be
growing when its snapshots are kept.

01:04:19.937 --> 01:04:23.507
Lengthen those runs,
or flag and exclude them?

01:04:25.137 --> 01:04:30.667
Near a gentle threshold, the truth rises
like a square root, vertical at first.

01:04:31.107 --> 01:04:33.539
A stiff machine rounds the corner;

01:04:33.619 --> 01:04:37.717
a supple one errs less,
but turns jumpy everywhere.

01:04:38.457 --> 01:04:42.377
Near a subcritical threshold,
two states coexist.

01:04:42.847 --> 01:04:46.349
In published Taylor-Green
runs, a dynamo, once on,

01:04:46.429 --> 01:04:49.767
survived down to a third of its threshold.

01:04:50.247 --> 01:04:53.657
Told only the knob, a machine blends them.

01:04:54.397 --> 01:04:59.587
The plan's bet: tell it the branch,
and its threshold error at least halves.

01:05:00.927 --> 01:05:06.927
Confidence: low; statistical stability
along branches is unproven in turbulence.

01:05:07.227 --> 01:05:12.777
No downward sweep is configured: from
which saturated runs, and how far down?

01:05:14.203 --> 01:05:16.153
Some things need no bet.

01:05:16.583 --> 01:05:19.645
In three dimensions,
hill maps become potentials:

01:05:19.725 --> 01:05:23.133
the generator draws them,
then takes their curl.

01:05:23.303 --> 01:05:26.919
So the flow and the field stay
divergence-free to rounding,

01:05:26.999 --> 01:05:29.083
even with untrained weights.

01:05:29.823 --> 01:05:34.433
Mirror the starting noise, and the
sample comes out with its field flipped.

01:05:34.893 --> 01:05:37.923
That shows the mechanism, not the quality.

01:05:38.970 --> 01:05:40.880
Everything else must be measured.

01:05:41.330 --> 01:05:44.901
First test: does the generator
get the energy spectra right,

01:05:44.981 --> 01:05:48.840
within a tenth of a decade,
at the settings it trained on?

01:05:49.580 --> 01:05:51.370
Confidence: low.

01:05:51.770 --> 01:05:55.637
In two dimensions, the best
models only just got there,

01:05:55.717 --> 01:06:01.430
and here each run keeps forty snapshots,
against five thousand in the plan.

01:06:03.770 --> 01:06:09.770
Now the crowd law in three dimensions:
energy in equals energy out, on average.

01:06:10.410 --> 01:06:13.927
In two dimensions, the crowd
loss cut the budget error

01:06:14.007 --> 01:06:18.600
to six percent; an equal-length
control run stayed at forty-six.

01:06:19.340 --> 01:06:22.483
The bet: under five percent
at held-out settings,

01:06:22.563 --> 01:06:26.840
the control higher,
the dissipation's spread within a fifth.

01:06:28.180 --> 01:06:32.083
Confidence: low. On three
dials, the loss shrank the

01:06:32.163 --> 01:06:36.860
spread of energy changes,
and failed from a biased start.

01:06:37.250 --> 01:06:43.250
Ask first: where runs may still be
growing, trust the data or the crowd law?

01:06:44.803 --> 01:06:48.741
Before the Taylor-Green runs, which
forcing, Reynolds number definition

01:06:48.821 --> 01:06:52.943
and resolution make their threshold
comparable with published ones?

01:06:55.170 --> 01:06:59.383
Two steps remain: the VKS
cylinder with its iron impellers,

01:06:59.463 --> 01:07:02.340
and DRESDYN, a precessing cylinder.

01:07:02.950 --> 01:07:08.950
First, what exact means once there are
walls, metals and meshes; then their bets.

01:07:12.070 --> 01:07:14.437
Before the walls, one convention:

01:07:14.517 --> 01:07:18.850
in the sodium experiment,
Rm has no single definition.

01:07:19.170 --> 01:07:23.896
One paper multiplies by an impeller
efficiency near zero point six,

01:07:23.976 --> 01:07:27.390
another does not,
a third uses other radii.

01:07:28.130 --> 01:07:33.140
So one threshold reads about thirty-one,
or fifty-two to seventy-one.

01:07:33.510 --> 01:07:37.600
A generator conditioned on
Rm must say which it means.

01:07:38.637 --> 01:07:39.927
Now the walls.

01:07:40.257 --> 01:07:42.636
Many potentials draw the same field:

01:07:42.716 --> 01:07:46.667
add the slope of any hill,
and the curl is unchanged.

01:07:47.137 --> 01:07:51.647
Choose among them without a fixed rule,
and the generator learns noise.

01:07:52.387 --> 01:07:58.387
So fix the gauge: no divergence inside,
no potential flowing through the wall.

01:07:58.807 --> 01:08:02.037
A published theorem then
gives each admissible

01:08:02.117 --> 01:08:06.117
field one potential: none
lost, none counted twice.

01:08:06.487 --> 01:08:08.542
If the field never crosses the wall,

01:08:08.622 --> 01:08:11.917
the potential may instead
meet it at right angles.

01:08:12.657 --> 01:08:16.610
One metric changes meaning:
where the field crosses the wall,

01:08:16.690 --> 01:08:21.847
magnetic helicity depends on the gauge,
so a relative helicity is needed.

01:08:22.870 --> 01:08:25.470
Now the iron that made VKS work.

01:08:25.840 --> 01:08:29.547
At its surface, the part of the
field crossing it is continuous,

01:08:29.627 --> 01:08:33.330
and so is the part along it,
divided by the permeability.

01:08:34.070 --> 01:08:37.070
Can a smooth potential obey both rules?

01:08:40.310 --> 01:08:43.556
No: a smooth potential
keeps the field continuous,

01:08:43.636 --> 01:08:48.290
breaking the second rule unless
the field meets the iron head-on.

01:08:48.710 --> 01:08:50.905
The plan proposes an untested fix:

01:08:50.985 --> 01:08:54.460
a potential deliberately
kinked at the surface.

01:08:55.200 --> 01:08:58.833
Outside, the field must
join a current-free field:

01:08:58.913 --> 01:09:04.070
linear but nonlocal, each wall
point depending on the whole wall.

01:09:04.440 --> 01:09:06.250
How to impose it is open.

01:09:07.303 --> 01:09:10.962
In the periodic box, two scalars,
poloidal and toroidal,

01:09:11.042 --> 01:09:14.243
can replace a potential's
three components.

01:09:14.703 --> 01:09:17.816
Their field is divergence-free,
with no gauge:

01:09:17.896 --> 01:09:20.863
the plan stores its box data that way.

01:09:21.603 --> 01:09:27.603
The partners' solver slices space instead:
around the axis, waves of zero,

01:09:27.690 --> 01:09:33.690
one, two, three bumps per turn;
across each half-plane, a triangle mesh.

01:09:34.683 --> 01:09:40.363
A continuous potential on that mesh gives
a divergence-free curl, wave by wave.

01:09:40.863 --> 01:09:43.957
But the solver's data are
only nearly divergence-free:

01:09:44.037 --> 01:09:46.953
the generator will be
cleaner than its data.

01:09:47.173 --> 01:09:49.393
Measure with the solver's yardstick.

01:09:51.837 --> 01:09:54.107
The VKS bet would train a generator on the

01:09:54.187 --> 01:09:57.147
partners' simulations of
the sodium experiment.

01:09:57.547 --> 01:10:01.131
In their reference run,
four patterns dominate:

01:10:01.211 --> 01:10:05.127
a dipole, a quadrupole,
and two flow patterns.

01:10:05.867 --> 01:10:08.815
The bet: their energy
shares within ten percent,

01:10:08.895 --> 01:10:10.644
and the distribution distance

01:10:10.724 --> 01:10:14.397
of dipole and quadrupole
under twice the noise floor.

01:10:14.737 --> 01:10:17.136
Plus the iron rule within one percent,

01:10:17.216 --> 01:10:21.787
and Rice's reversal rate inside
the simulated count's error bars.

01:10:23.027 --> 01:10:26.108
Confidence: low. The data are not public,

01:10:26.188 --> 01:10:31.607
no file reader exists yet, and few
reversals mean wide error bars.

01:10:32.067 --> 01:10:37.327
Worth asking first: how many snapshots
per configuration, and by when?

01:10:38.770 --> 01:10:44.500
The DRESDYN bet: a sodium cylinder
spinning while its axis slowly turns.

01:10:44.870 --> 01:10:50.760
Its knob: the precession ratio, how fast
the axis turns relative to the spin.

01:10:51.500 --> 01:10:54.600
The generator draws only
the flow; a solver finds the

01:10:54.680 --> 01:10:58.670
threshold and the symmetry of
the field that flow would grow.

01:10:58.940 --> 01:11:04.850
The bet: both right at an unseen ratio,
the threshold within ten percent.

01:11:06.090 --> 01:11:11.790
Confidence: low. Few ratios are planned,
and this step would be dropped first.

01:11:12.150 --> 01:11:16.680
Worth asking now: how many ratios,
and how close together?

01:11:18.137 --> 01:11:21.183
Downstream, the generator
feeds reduced models:

01:11:21.263 --> 01:11:25.241
their best drift averages the
equations' drift over snapshots

01:11:25.321 --> 01:11:28.777
sharing a reduced state,
no time series needed.

01:11:29.517 --> 01:11:31.457
It also serves as a prior.

01:11:31.727 --> 01:11:35.607
A probe reads the dipole
near zero, mid-reversal.

01:11:36.187 --> 01:11:38.977
Where does the average of
the matching states sit?

01:11:42.217 --> 01:11:46.377
Between two mirror humps,
where the dynamo almost never is.

01:11:46.897 --> 01:11:50.855
Methods keeping only a mean
and a spread chase that ghost;

01:11:50.935 --> 01:11:54.537
a lopsided prior would
wrongly favour one hump.

01:11:55.037 --> 01:11:58.956
A built-in mirror handles it
exactly: sample mirror pairs,

01:11:59.036 --> 01:12:01.917
or search from a code and its mirror.

01:12:03.270 --> 01:12:08.172
Which labels must each VKS snapshot
carry: which Rm definition,

01:12:08.252 --> 01:12:12.220
which wall condition,
which iron permeability?

01:12:15.670 --> 01:12:18.858
The bill: about eleven
thousand graphics-card hours,

01:12:18.938 --> 01:12:22.410
counting a twenty percent
reserve for failed runs.

01:12:22.870 --> 01:12:25.820
The three-dimensional box
takes the biggest share.

01:12:26.160 --> 01:12:29.362
Most lines are scaled from
one published training run:

01:12:29.442 --> 01:12:32.020
about eleven hundred and fifty hours.

01:12:32.760 --> 01:12:35.210
No graphics-card hour
has been measured yet.

01:12:35.800 --> 01:12:40.390
The bet: measured costs land
within a factor of two of the plan.

01:12:41.630 --> 01:12:47.110
Confidence: low; the plan's own
error bar is at least twofold.

01:12:47.500 --> 01:12:51.489
Ask first: the reference
simulations, twenty thousand

01:12:51.569 --> 01:12:56.660
processor hours or more, are off
this bill. On whose machines?

01:12:58.903 --> 01:13:04.903
The whole plan on one page:
thirty-six months, three axes, five steps.

01:13:05.093 --> 01:13:08.976
Hard constraints, crowd laws,
thresholds and reversals,

01:13:09.056 --> 01:13:12.413
from three dials to a precessing cylinder.

01:13:12.633 --> 01:13:15.902
Three papers, at months
ten, twenty-two and

01:13:15.982 --> 01:13:19.583
thirty-three, with no acceptance promised.

01:13:20.323 --> 01:13:23.368
It merges the two best-scored
of six directions;

01:13:23.448 --> 01:13:27.033
they stay on top under all
three weightings tried.

01:13:27.593 --> 01:13:32.100
Among its named risks:
non-public data, a bill over budget,

01:13:32.180 --> 01:13:37.953
an unstable crowd loss in three
dimensions, the error near thresholds.

01:13:38.123 --> 01:13:43.243
One risk it cannot yet rate:
funding after MilaDy's nominal end.

01:13:44.383 --> 01:13:49.283
An independent proposal, not requested
or validated by the supervisors.

01:13:50.837 --> 01:13:54.451
A thesis starting in fall
twenty twenty-seven would end

01:13:54.531 --> 01:13:58.487
after MilaDy's nominal end:
who funds its last months?

01:14:03.537 --> 01:14:06.457
Every chapter so far
has ended on a question.

01:14:06.787 --> 01:14:08.917
The empty seats raised more.

01:14:09.657 --> 01:14:12.217
Hang each one under the step it shapes.

01:14:12.687 --> 01:14:17.107
Bet one: whether to add settings
between seventeen and eighteen.

01:14:17.617 --> 01:14:22.657
Bet two: the seed, the Reynolds
number, the length of each run.

01:14:22.947 --> 01:14:25.337
The generator: what it must match.

01:14:25.557 --> 01:14:28.177
The top steps: the partners' data.

01:14:28.917 --> 01:14:32.109
A staircase of bets is a
staircase of decisions,

01:14:32.189 --> 01:14:35.177
and the first ones come before any run.

01:14:37.003 --> 01:14:41.443
First, the runs: these answers
change what gets computed.

01:14:42.183 --> 01:14:47.643
Near a threshold, start from a
tiny seed, a strong one, or both?

01:14:48.213 --> 01:14:51.933
Which values of Pm, since
sodium's is out of reach?

01:14:52.153 --> 01:14:55.783
Settings spread evenly,
or packed near each threshold?

01:14:56.263 --> 01:14:58.803
How many independent
snapshots per setting?

01:14:59.233 --> 01:15:02.243
How long must runs near a threshold last?

01:15:02.923 --> 01:15:05.907
Which forcing, Reynolds
number and grid make the

01:15:05.987 --> 01:15:09.763
Taylor-Green threshold
comparable with published ones?

01:15:10.143 --> 01:15:12.253
And which campaign comes first?

01:15:13.303 --> 01:15:19.183
Then the framing: how each result will
be judged, decided before the data exist.

01:15:19.603 --> 01:15:23.609
Which statistics must generated
fields match, down to the shape of

01:15:23.689 --> 01:15:28.353
their field lines, and how close to
the noise floor counts as success?

01:15:28.863 --> 01:15:33.363
Which budgets must hold on average,
and must their spread match too?

01:15:34.403 --> 01:15:39.103
One answer, not about physics,
could make most renting unnecessary.

01:15:39.453 --> 01:15:40.763
Which question is it?

01:15:44.003 --> 01:15:45.703
Where the computing happens.

01:15:46.113 --> 01:15:50.378
If a lab has graphics cards an
intern can use, or there is already

01:15:50.458 --> 01:15:54.803
a Jean Zay project to join,
renting shrinks to a short trial.

01:15:55.153 --> 01:16:01.133
And for the top steps: which partner
simulations, in what form, and by when?

01:16:02.170 --> 01:16:04.823
The second shelf matters
for the internship plan

01:16:04.903 --> 01:16:08.080
and the data request,
not for the first campaigns.

01:16:08.690 --> 01:16:12.020
Which settings get runs long
enough to count reversals?

01:16:12.340 --> 01:16:15.570
Which operator decides what exact means?

01:16:15.780 --> 01:16:18.480
Which labels must each snapshot carry?

01:16:18.750 --> 01:16:21.260
And may new results appear in this film?

01:16:22.000 --> 01:16:28.000
The last shelf can wait: the top steps'
details, and how to frame a first paper.

01:16:29.137 --> 01:16:33.937
The first shelf fits in one message,
to write before renting anything.

01:16:34.367 --> 01:16:36.487
This ranking is only a proposal.

01:16:36.697 --> 01:16:38.827
The supervisors may well change it.

01:16:39.567 --> 01:16:41.927
Several answers could
change what gets computed,

01:16:42.007 --> 01:16:44.217
or whether anything is rented at all.

01:16:44.587 --> 01:16:46.990
The cheapest experiment
is a good question,

01:16:47.070 --> 01:16:49.537
asked before renting a graphics card.

01:16:54.970 --> 01:16:57.280
Questions first, then computing.

01:16:57.550 --> 01:17:00.860
Here is what those questions
rest on, in two columns.

01:17:01.230 --> 01:17:06.190
On the left, what three small experiments
measured on an ordinary processor.

01:17:06.360 --> 01:17:08.702
Toy problems, one or two training seeds,

01:17:08.782 --> 01:17:13.170
and machines tested only between
the settings they were trained on.

01:17:13.910 --> 01:17:18.890
On the right, what the staircase bets,
each with the rule that will settle it.

01:17:19.670 --> 01:17:23.334
Each lamp shows a verdict only
once its data file arrives,

01:17:23.414 --> 01:17:25.810
and the film is updated with it.

01:17:27.437 --> 01:17:30.347
Back to the compass,
and to the two questions.

01:17:31.587 --> 01:17:33.357
When will this needle turn?

01:17:33.687 --> 01:17:35.997
For the Earth, no one can say.

01:17:36.517 --> 01:17:39.182
In a simple computer
model, chaos makes the

01:17:39.262 --> 01:17:42.337
forecast fade before
the average wait ends.

01:17:42.747 --> 01:17:48.417
But how often the field flips, counted as
crossings of zero, can still be answered.

01:17:48.927 --> 01:17:53.757
The answer hides in a shoebox of
snapshots, read with the equations.

01:17:54.377 --> 01:17:59.967
And a machine can learn to dream such
snapshots, between the simulated settings.

01:18:01.107 --> 01:18:04.007
On small test problems, it partly works.

01:18:04.277 --> 01:18:07.407
The mirror holds by construction,
at every setting.

01:18:07.687 --> 01:18:10.270
On three dials, the rate of zero crossings

01:18:10.350 --> 01:18:14.357
is roughly right far from the
threshold, and poor near it.

01:18:14.657 --> 01:18:17.927
A smooth machine rounds
off a steep change.

01:18:18.107 --> 01:18:21.036
The bigger tests,
in three dimensions and in the

01:18:21.116 --> 01:18:24.267
shapes of real machines,
are written as bets.

01:18:25.607 --> 01:18:27.147
One lesson remains.

01:18:27.367 --> 01:18:29.947
Enforce each law where it is true.

01:18:30.217 --> 01:18:32.767
Exactly, where it is exact.

01:18:33.057 --> 01:18:36.597
On average, where it
only holds on average.

01:18:40.237 --> 01:18:43.416
The report, the code,
and every result in this film are

01:18:43.496 --> 01:18:46.967
on the project's site,
with their limits written out.

01:18:47.457 --> 01:18:50.367
The rest of the staircase
is still to be computed.
